WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.320
Today I'd like to tell you about a piece&nbsp;
of math known as holomorphic dynamics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.560
This is the field which studies things like the&nbsp;
Mandelbrot set, and in fact one of my main goals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:14.160
today is to show you how this iconic shape, the&nbsp;
poster child of math, pops up in a more general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.520
way than the initial definition might suggest.&nbsp;
This field is also intimately tied to what we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.240
talked about in the last video with Newton's&nbsp;
fractal, and another goal of ours towards the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.840
end of this video will be to help tie up&nbsp;
some of the loose ends that we had there.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.880
So, first of all, this word "holomorphic" might&nbsp;
seem a little weird. It refers to functions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:44.160
have complex number inputs and complex number&nbsp;
outputs and which you can also take a derivative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:49.040
of. Basically what it means to have a derivative&nbsp;
in this context is that when you zoom in to how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.000
the function behaves near a given point, to&nbsp;
the point and its neighbors, it looks roughly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.800
like scaling and rotating, like multiplying by&nbsp;
some complex constant. We'll talk more about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.720
that in just a bit, but for now know that it&nbsp;
includes most of the ordinary functions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:06.560
you could write down, things like polynomials,&nbsp;
exponentials, trig functions, all of that.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:12.960
The relevant dynamics in the title here comes from&nbsp;
asking what happens when you repeatedly apply one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:18.640
of these functions over and over, in the sense of&nbsp;
evaluating on some input then evaluating the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:25.840
function on whatever you just got out, and then&nbsp;
doing that again and again and again and again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:30.080
Sometimes the pattern of points emerging&nbsp;
from this gets trapped in a cycle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:34.560
other times the sequence will just&nbsp;
approach some kind of limiting point.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:40.640
Or maybe the sequence gets bigger and bigger and&nbsp;
it flies off to infinity, which mathematicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.840
also kind of think of as approaching a&nbsp;
limit point, just the "point at infinity".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:48.720
And other times still they have no pattern&nbsp;
at all, and they behave chaotically.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.160
What's surprising is that for all sorts&nbsp;
of functions that you might write down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.960
when you try to do something to visualize&nbsp;
when these different possible behaviors arise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:02.160
it often results in some insanely&nbsp;
intricate fractal pattern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.120
Those of you who watched the last video&nbsp;
have already seen one neat example of this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.760
There's this algorithm called Newton's method&nbsp;
which finds the root of some polynomial p,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.480
and the way it works is to basically repeatedly&nbsp;
iterate the expression x -p(x)/p'(x), p' being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:24.080
the derivative. When your initial seed value is in&nbsp;
the loose vicinity of a root to that polynomial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.480
a value where p(x)=0, this procedure&nbsp;
produces a sequence of values that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:32.560
really quickly converges to that root. This is&nbsp;
what makes it a useful algorithm in practice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:38.400
But then we tried to do this in the complex&nbsp;
plane, looking at the many possible seed values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.400
and asking which root in the complex plane&nbsp;
each one of these seed values might end up on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:48.160
then we associated a color with each one of the&nbsp;
roots and colored each pixel of the plane based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.480
on which root a seed value starting at that pixel&nbsp;
would ultimately land on, the results we got were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.200
some of these insanely intricate pictures with&nbsp;
these rough fractal boundaries between the colors.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.320
In this example, if you look at the function&nbsp;
that we're actually iterating, say for some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:12.320
specific choice of a polynomial like z^3 - 1, you&nbsp;
can rewrite the whole expression to look like one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:17.840
polynomial divided by another. Mathematicians call&nbsp;
these kinds of functions "rational functions",&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.280
and if you forget the fact that&nbsp;
this arose from Newton's method,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.360
you could reasonably ask what happens when&nbsp;
you iterate any other rational function.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.040
In fact, this is exactly what the mathematicians&nbsp;
Piere Fatou and Gaston Julia did in the years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.040
immediately following World War 1, and&nbsp;
they built up a surprisingly rich theory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:40.080
of what happens when you iterate these rational&nbsp;
functions, which is particularly impressive given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.160
that they had no computers to visualize&nbsp;
any of this the way that you and I can.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:48.400
I think this distinguishes Julia as easily&nbsp;
being one of the greatest mathematicians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:52.560
all time who had no nose. Remember those&nbsp;
two names, they'll come up a bit later.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:58.400
By far the most popularized example of a rational&nbsp;
function that you might study like this and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:05.120
fractals that can ensue is one of the simplest&nbsp;
functions, z^2 + c, where c is some constant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.320
I'm going to guess that this is at least somewhat&nbsp;
familiar to many of you but it certainly doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:14.640
hurt to quickly summarize the story here since&nbsp;
it can help set the stage for what comes later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:20.240
For this game, we're going to think of c as a&nbsp;
value that can be changed and it'll be visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.520
as this movable yellow dot. For the actual&nbsp;
iterative process, we will always start with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:34.400
an initial value of z=0. So after iterating this&nbsp;
function once, doing z^2 + c, you get c. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:40.320
iterate a second time, plugging in that value&nbsp;
to the function, you get c squared plus c. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.720
as I change around the value c here you can kind&nbsp;
of see how the second value moves in lockstep.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.640
Then we can plug in that second value to&nbsp;
get z_3 and that third value to get z_4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:55.840
and continue on like this&nbsp;
visualizing our chain of values.

00:04:56.480 --> 00:05:01.440
If I keep doing this many different times for&nbsp;
the first many values, for some choices of c&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.240
this process remains bounded, you can still see&nbsp;
it all on the screen, and other times it looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.720
like it blows up. And you can actually show that&nbsp;
if it gets as big as 2, it'll blow up to infinity.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:19.120
If you color the points of the plane where it&nbsp;
stays bounded black and you assign some other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:25.440
gradient of colors to the divergent values based&nbsp;
on how quickly the process rushes off to infinity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.520
you get one of the most iconic images in all of&nbsp;
math, the Mandelbrot set. now this interactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:36.240
dots and stick visualization of the trajectory,&nbsp;
by the way, is heavily inspired by Ben Sparks'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.160
illustration in the Numberphile video he did&nbsp;
about the Mandelbrot set, which is great,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:43.920
you should watch it. I honestly thought it&nbsp;
was just too fun not to re-implement here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.920 --> 00:05:48.160
I would also highly recommend the interactive&nbsp;
article on Acko.net about all of this stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.640
for any of you who haven't had&nbsp;
the pleasure of reading that yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:55.600
What's nice about the Ben Sparks illustration&nbsp;
is how it illuminates what each different part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:01.760
of the mandelbrot set actually represents. This&nbsp;
largest cardioid section includes the values of c&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.760 --> 00:06:06.720
so that the process eventually converges&nbsp;
to some limit. The big circle on the left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:11.760
represents the values where the process gets&nbsp;
trapped in a cycle between two values. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:16.000
the top and bottom circles show values where the&nbsp;
process gets trapped in a cycle of three values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:19.840
and so on like this. Each one of these&nbsp;
little islands kind of has its own meaning.

00:06:22.640 --> 00:06:26.400
Also notice, there is an important&nbsp;
difference between how this Mandelbrot set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:29.600
and the Newton fractals we were&nbsp;
looking at before are each constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:31.600
beyond just a different underlying function.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:38.160
For the mandelbrot set we have a consistent seed&nbsp;
value z=zero, but the thing we're tweaking is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.640
parameter c, changing the function itself. So&nbsp;
what you're looking at is what we might call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:47.840
a "Parameter space". With Newton's fractal,&nbsp;
we have a single unchanging function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:52.240
but what we associate with each pixel is&nbsp;
a different seed value for the process.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.200
Of course we could play the&nbsp;
same game with the map z^2 + c.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:01.520
We could fix c at some constant and then let&nbsp;
the pixels represent the different possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:07.440
initial values z_0. Wo whereas each pixel of the&nbsp;
Mandelbrot set corresponds to a unique function,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:10.880
the images on the right each just&nbsp;
correspond to a single function.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:16.880
As we change the parameter c, it changes the&nbsp;
entire image on the right. And again, just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:22.560
be clear, the rule being applied is that we color&nbsp;
pixels black if the process remains bounded and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.600
then apply some kind of gradient to the ones that&nbsp;
diverge away to infinity based on how quickly they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.600 --> 00:07:32.960
diverge to infinity. In principle, and it's kind&nbsp;
of mind-warping to think about, there is some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:38.400
four-dimensional space of all combinations of&nbsp;
c and z_0, and what we're doing here is kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:45.040
looking through individual two-dimensional slices&nbsp;
of that unimaginable pattern. You'll often hear or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:50.480
read the images on the right being referred to&nbsp;
as "Julia sets" or "Julia fractals". And when I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:54.240
first learned about all this stuff, I'll admit&nbsp;
that I kind of was left with the misconception&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.240 --> 00:08:00.000
that this is what the term Julia set refers to,&nbsp;
specifically the z^2 + c case, and moreover that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:04.560
it's referring to the black region on the inside.&nbsp;
However the term "Julia set" has a much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.360
general definition, and it would refer just to&nbsp;
the boundaries of these regions, not the interior.

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:13.840
To set the stage for a more specific&nbsp;
definition, and to also make some headway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.080
towards the first goal that I mentioned at the&nbsp;
start, it's worth stepping back and really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.400
picturing yourself as a mathematician right&nbsp;
now discovering all of this. What would you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:27.520
actually do to construct a theory around this?&nbsp;
It's one thing to look at some pretty pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.040
but what sorts of questions would you ask&nbsp;
if you actually want to understand it all?

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:36.400
In general if you want to understand something&nbsp;
complicated, a good place to start is to ask if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:41.120
there are any parts of the system that have some&nbsp;
simple behavior, preferably the simplest possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:47.440
behavior. In our example, that might mean asking&nbsp;
when does the process just stay fixed in place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.160
meaning f(z) = z. That's a pretty boring&nbsp;
set of dynamics, I think you'd agree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.200
We call a value with this property a "fixed&nbsp;
point" of the function. In the case of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.240
functions arising from Newton's method, by&nbsp;
design they have a fixed point at the roots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:09.040
of the relevant polynomial. You can verify for&nbsp;
yourself if p(z) = 0, then the entire expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:14.160
is simply equal to z. That's what it means to be&nbsp;
a fixed point. If you're into exercises, you may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:21.840
enjoy pausing for a moment and computing the fixed&nbsp;
points of this Mandelbrot set function, z^2 + c.

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:27.840
More generally, any rational function will&nbsp;
always have fixed points, since asking when this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:32.720
expression equals z can always be rearranged as&nbsp;
finding the roots of some polynomial expression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:36.880
and from the fundamental theorem of&nbsp;
algebra this must have solutions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:40.080
typically as many solutions as the&nbsp;
highest degree in this expression.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:44.720
Incidentally this means that you&nbsp;
could also find those fixed points&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:49.200
using Newton's method, but maybe that's&nbsp;
a little bit too meta for us right now.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.960
Just asking about fixed points is maybe&nbsp;
easy, but a key idea for understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:56.240
the full dynamics, and hence the&nbsp;
diagrams that we're looking at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:01.360
is to understand stability. We say&nbsp;
that a fixed point is attracting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.360 --> 00:10:06.640
if nearby points tend to get drawn in towards&nbsp;
it, and repelling if they're pushed away. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:11.040
this is something that you can actually compute&nbsp;
explicitly using the derivative of the function.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:16.000
Symbolically, when you take derivatives&nbsp;
of complex functions, it looks exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:20.640
the same as it would for real functions. So&nbsp;
something like z^2 has a derivative of 2z.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:26.880
But geometrically there's a really lovely way to&nbsp;
interpret what this means. For example, at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:32.800
input 1 the derivative of this particular function&nbsp;
evaluates to be 2, and what that's telling us is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:37.680
that if you look at a very small neighborhood&nbsp;
around that input and you follow what happens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:41.440
to all the points in that little neighborhood&nbsp;
as you apply the function, in this case z^2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.640 --> 00:10:47.680
then it looks just like you're multiplying&nbsp;
by 2. This is what a derivative of 2 means.

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:55.120
To take another example let's look at the input i.&nbsp;
We know that this function moves that input to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.120 --> 00:11:02.320
value -1, that's i^2. But the added information&nbsp;
that its derivative at this value is 2 times i&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:06.880
gives us the added picture that when you zoom in&nbsp;
around that point and you look at the action of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.880 --> 00:11:12.800
the function on this tiny neighborhood, it looks&nbsp;
like multiplication by 2i, which in this case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.800 --> 00:11:17.440
is saying it looks like a 90 degree rotation&nbsp;
combined with a expansion by a factor of 2.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.200
For the purposes of analyzing stability,&nbsp;
the only thing we care about here is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:30.320
growing and shrinking factor, the rotational part&nbsp;
doesn't matter. So if you compute the derivative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.440
of a function at its fixed point, and the&nbsp;
absolute value of this result is less than one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:40.000
it tells you that the fixed point is attracting,&nbsp;
that nearby points tend to come in towards it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:43.920
If that derivative has an absolute&nbsp;
value bigger than one, it tells you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:46.640
the fixed point is repelling,&nbsp;
it pushes away its neighbors.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.720
For example if you work out the derivative of&nbsp;
our Newton's map expression, and you simplify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:58.560
a couple things a little bit, here's what&nbsp;
you would get out. So if z is a fixed point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.000
which in this context means that it's&nbsp;
one of the roots of the polynomial p,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.000
this derivative is not only smaller&nbsp;
than one, it's equal to zero.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:11.280
These are sometimes called "superattracting" fixed&nbsp;
points, since it means that a neighborhood around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:17.040
these points doesn't merely shrink, it shrinks&nbsp;
a lot. And again this is kind of by design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.040 --> 00:12:22.640
since the intent of Newton's method is to produce&nbsp;
iterations that fall towards a root as quickly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:28.800
they can. Pulling up our z^2 + c, example if you&nbsp;
did the first exercise to find its fixed points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:34.000
the next step would be to ask when is at&nbsp;
least one of those fixed points is attracting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:39.600
For what values of c is this going to be true?&nbsp;
And then if that's not enough of a challenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:45.040
try using the result that you find to show that&nbsp;
this condition corresponds to the main cardioid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.040 --> 00:12:49.280
shape of the Mandelbrot set. This is something&nbsp;
you can compute explicitly, it's pretty cool.

00:12:51.440 --> 00:12:54.240
A natural next step would be to ask about cycles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.240 --> 00:12:56.240
and this is where things really&nbsp;
start to get interesting.

00:12:57.040 --> 00:13:03.280
If f(z) is not z, but some other value, and then&nbsp;
that value comes back to z, it means that you've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:09.440
fallen into a two-cycle. You could explicitly&nbsp;
find these kinds of two-cycles by evaluating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.440 --> 00:13:15.760
f(f(z)), and then setting it equal to z.&nbsp;
For example with the z^2 + c map, f(f(z))&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:21.680
expands out to look like this. It's a little&nbsp;
messy, but you know it's not too terrible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.680 --> 00:13:25.680
The main thing to highlight is that it boils&nbsp;
down to solving some degree four equation.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:30.240
You should note though that the fixed points&nbsp;
will also be solutions to this equation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:35.280
so technically the two-cycles are the solutions&nbsp;
to this minus the solutions to the original fixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:41.680
point equation. And likewise you can use the same&nbsp;
idea to look for n-cycles by composing f with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:48.080
itself n different times. The explicit expressions&nbsp;
that you would get quickly become insanely messy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:53.280
but it's still elucidating to ask how many cycles&nbsp;
would you expect based on this hypothetical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.280 --> 00:13:58.960
process. If we stick with our simple z^2 +&nbsp;
c example, as you compose it with itself you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.960 --> 00:14:05.200
get a polynomial with degree 4, and then one with&nbsp;
degree 8, and then degree 16, and so on and so on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:11.200
exponentially growing the order of the polynomial.&nbsp;
So in principle, if I asked you how many cycles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.080
are there with a period of one million, you&nbsp;
can know that it's equivalent to solving some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:21.760
just absolutely insane polynomial expression&nbsp;
with a degree of 2 to the one million. So again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.480
fundamental theorem of algebra, you would expect&nbsp;
to find something on the order of 2 to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.480 --> 00:14:32.720
one million points in the complex plane which&nbsp;
cycle in exactly this way. And more generally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:37.440
for any rational map, you'll always be able to&nbsp;
find values whose behavior falls into a cycle with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.440 --> 00:14:42.800
period n. It ultimately boils down to solving&nbsp;
some (probably insane) polynomial expression,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:48.880
and just like with this example the number of such&nbsp;
periodic points will grow exponentially with n.

00:14:50.160 --> 00:14:53.200
I didn't really talk about this in&nbsp;
the last video about newton's fractal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

