try to visualize which cubic polynomials have&nbsp;
attracting cycles at all. Hopefully, if Newton's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:39.120
method is going to be at all decent at finding&nbsp;
roots, those attracting cycles should be rare.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:44.800
First of all, to better visualize the one example&nbsp;
we're looking at, we could draw the same fractal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.800 --> 00:16:49.280
that we had before, coloring each point based on&nbsp;
what root the seed value starting at that point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.320
will tend to, but this time we'll have an added&nbsp;
condition of coloring points that says that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.320 --> 00:17:00.400
the seed value never gets close enough to a root&nbsp;
at all we will color the pixel black. Notice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:04.800
if I tweak the roots, meaning that we're&nbsp;
trying out different cubic polynomials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

